window.QUESTION_HINTS = {"cie-9701-2021-fm-p12-q3": "Start with the carbon in the CO₂:\nn(C) = 3.22 ÷ 44 = 0.0732 mol, m(C) = 0.0732 × 12 = 0.878 g\nThe rest is hydrogen: m(H) = 1.00 − 0.878 = 0.122 g, n(H) = 0.122 mol\nC : H = 0.0732 : 0.122 ≈ 3 : 5, i.e. C₆H₁₀\nFormulae of the options: cyclohexene C₆H₁₀, cyclopentane C₅H₁₀, ethene C₂H₄, pentane C₅H₁₂", "cie-9701-2021-mj-p11-q19": "5.00 g of the oxide contains 2.50 g of T, i.e. T is 50% by mass.\nWork out the percentage by mass of T in each oxide:\nMgO = 24.3/40.3 = 60%\nAl₂O₃ = 54/102 = 53%\nSiO₂ = 28.1/60.1 = 47%\nSO₂ = 32.1/64.1 = 50%", "cie-9701-2021-mj-p12-q32": "A straight-chain hydrocarbon is either an alkane or an alkene, so try both general formulae:\nalkane CnH2n+2: 14n + 2 = 84 → n = 5.86 (not a whole number, so no)\nalkene CnH2n: 14n = 84 → n = 6, i.e. C₆H₁₂\nSo the molecular formula and the empirical formula (CH₂) can both be found, and straight-chain C₆H₁₂ must contain one C=C. All three statements are true.", "cie-9701-2021-mj-p13-q9": "Balancing only. Write the equation and read the coefficients:\n3Cu + 8HNO₃ → 3Cu(NO₃)₂ + 4H₂O + 2NO, so 3 mol Cu needs 8 mol of acid", "cie-9701-2021-on-p11-q1": "Every peak is one isotope: the x-axis is the mass number and the number in brackets is the relative abundance.\nUse the weighted mean (same as IGCSE Ar):\nAr = (20×100 + 21×8 + 22×0.3) ÷ (100 + 8 + 0.3)", "cie-9701-2021-on-p11-q28": "First count the -COOH groups in the structure:\nHO₂CCH₂C(OH)(CO₂H)CH₂CO₂H has 3, so it is triprotic\nEach -COOH neutralises one NaOH: n(NaOH) = 3 × 0.0050 = 0.0150 mol\nV = 0.0150 ÷ 0.40 = 0.0375 dm³ = 37.5 cm³", "cie-9701-2022-fm-p12-q23": "Same equation as Homework 2 Q7:\n3Cl₂ + 6NaOH → 5NaCl + NaClO₃ + 3H₂O\nRead the coefficients: 6 mol NaOH gives 5 mol NaCl, so n(NaCl) = (5/6) × n(NaOH)\nn(NaOH) = 0.100 × 6.0 = 0.600 mol, giving n(NaCl) = 0.500 mol", "cie-9701-2022-fm-p12-q31": "The product is given: refluxing ethanol with acidified K₂Cr₂O₇ gives ethanoic acid (not ethanal):\nCH₃CH₂OH → CH₃COOH, Mr = 60.0\nn(C₂H₅OH) = 2.30 ÷ 46 = 0.0500 mol (1:1)\nTheoretical yield = 0.0500 × 60.0 = 3.00 g; then multiply by 60.0%", "cie-9701-2022-mj-p12-q4": "Combustion analysis only:\nn(C) = 3.52 ÷ 44 = 0.0800 mol\nn(H) = 2 × (1.44 ÷ 18) = 0.160 mol\nC : H = 1 : 2, so Q must have the formula CnH2n (twice as many H as C)\nCount the C and H atoms in structures A-D and choose the one with H = 2 × C", "cie-9701-2022-mj-p13-q21": "The equation is given:\nM(NO₃)₂ → MO + 2NO₂ + ½O₂, and the solid left is MO\n4.00 ÷ (Ar + 124) = 1.37 ÷ (Ar + 16), giving Ar ≈ 40.3", "cie-9701-2023-mj-p11-q26": "First turn the names into molecular formulae; then it is just a balancing question:\npropane C₃H₈ | propanoic acid C₃H₆O₂ | propanone C₃H₆O (= CH₃COCH₃) | propan-1-ol C₃H₈O\nCombustion: CaHbOc + (a + b/4 − c/2) O₂ → a CO₂ + (b/2) H₂O\nSubstituting (a + b/4 − c/2), only D gives 3 + 2 − 0.5 = 4.5", "cie-9701-2023-on-p11-q21": "The equation is given:\nM(NO₃)₂ → MO + 2NO₂ + ½O₂\nKey point: only gas escapes on heating, so the solid left is MO; residue = 5.00 − 3.29 = 1.71 g\nThe same salt gives two expressions for n that must be equal:\n5.00 ÷ (Ar + 124) = 1.71 ÷ (Ar + 16), giving Ar ≈ 40", "cie-9701-2023-on-p11-q25": "The molecular formula is given: hexamine is C₆H₁₂N₄\nThe empirical formula is the simplest whole-number ratio - divide by 2: C₃H₆N₂", "cie-9701-2024-mj-p11-q20": "The equation is given:\nMg(NO₃)₂ → MgO + 2NO₂ + ½O₂\nEach 1 mol of salt gives 2 + 0.5 = 2.5 mol of gas\nn(gas) = 0.010 × 2.5 = 0.0250 mol\nV = 0.0250 × 24.0 = 0.600 dm³ = 600 cm³", "cie-9701-2024-mj-p13-q7": "You only need to substitute into a formula. Ideal gas equation: pV = nRT, with R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.\nCombining n = pV/(RT) with Mr = m/n gives: Mr = mRT / (pV)\nSubstitute m = 0.10 g, T = 400 K, p = 101 300 Pa and V = 85 cm³ = 85 × 10⁻⁶ m³\n(convert cm³ to m³)", "cie-9701-2025-mj-p11-q12": "The equation is given: S₂O₆²⁻ → SO₂ + SO₄²⁻\nSO₄²⁻ gives a white precipitate of BaSO₄ with Ba²⁺, so the amount of precipitate equals the amount of BaS₂O₆ that has decomposed\nn(BaSO₄) = 0.661 ÷ 233.4 = 2.83 × 10⁻³ mol\nInitial n(BaS₂O₆·2H₂O) = 3.513 ÷ 333.5 = 1.053 × 10⁻² mol\nRemaining = 1.053×10⁻² − 2.83×10⁻³ = 7.70×10⁻³ mol (in 0.100 dm³), so c = 0.0770 mol dm⁻³", "cie-9701-2025-mj-p11-q6": "Two steps.\n(1) From the combustion data find the empirical formula: m(O) = 2.20 − 1.20 − 0.20 = 0.80 g;\nn(C):n(H):n(O) = 1.20/12 : 0.20/1 : 0.80/16 = 2 : 4 : 1, so the empirical formula is C₂H₄O.\n(2) Fizzing with Na₂CO₃ solution means a -COOH group (a carboxylic acid releases CO₂ from a carbonate).\nChoose the option with empirical formula C₂H₄O that contains -COOH.", "cie-9701-2025-mj-p13-q26": "The product is given:\nCH₃CN + NaOH + H₂O → CH₃COONa + NH₃\nSo S is sodium ethanoate, CH₃COONa, Mr = 82.0\nTheoretical yield = 0.200 × 82.0 = 16.4 g; then multiply by the 80.0% yield", "cie-9701-2025-mj-p14-q29": "Work out how much hydrogen is added:\nn(H₂) = 0.720 ÷ 24.0 = 0.0300 mol, n(X) = 0.010 mol, so each X adds 3 H₂\nRemember: each C=C adds 1 H₂ and each C≡C adds 2 H₂\nCount the double bonds (each worth 1) and triple bonds (each worth 2) in A-D; the one totalling 3 is the answer", "cie-9701-2025-mj-p14-q37": "Citric acid has 3 -COOH groups, so it is triprotic.\nNa₂CO₃ + 2H⁺ → 2Na⁺ + H₂O + CO₂, i.e. 1 mol Na₂CO₃ uses 2 mol H⁺\nn(Na₂CO₃) = 0.120 × 0.50 = 0.0600 mol, so n(H⁺) = 0.120 mol and n(citric acid) = 0.120 ÷ 3 = 0.0400 mol", "cie-9701-2025-on-p12-q21": "The equation is given:\n3Cl₂ + 6NaOH → 5NaCl + NaClO₃ + 3H₂O\nRead the coefficients: 3 mol Cl₂ gives 1 mol NaClO₃, so n(NaClO₃) = 0.600 ÷ 3 = 0.200 mol\nMr(NaClO₃) = 23 + 35.5 + 3×16 = 106.5"};
